\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does every complete Riemannian manifold of finite volume have a nonconstant closed geodesic? Must it in fact have infinitely many geometrically distinct closed geodesics? The source additionally asks analogous questions for finite-volume Finsler metrics, distinguishing reversible metrics and the noncompact irreversible case.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/geodesicsproblems.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every complete Riemannian manifold of finite volume have a nonconstant closed geodesic? Must it in fact have infinitely many geometrically distinct closed geodesics? The source additionally asks analogous questions for finite-volume Finsler metrics, distinguishing reversible metrics and the noncompact irreversible case.

